Background and setup. Fix the description logic DL-Lite_R, whose signature consists of atomic concepts and atomic roles, and whose TBox axioms are inclusions B_1 ⊑ B_2, B_1 ⊑ ¬B_2 between basic concepts (atomic concepts or expressions of the form ∃R, ∃R^-), and analogous inclusions/disjointness statements between basic roles. A DL-Lite_R knowledge base K = (T, A) consists of a TBox T and an ABox A of ground concept/role assertions.

A knowledge-base exchange setting is a tuple Σ = (Σ_1, Σ_2, T_1, T_2, M), where Σ_1, Σ_2 are disjoint source and target signatures, T_i is a DL-Lite_R TBox over Σ_i, and M is a finite set of mapping assertions of the form ∀x (φ(x) → ψ(x)) with φ a conjunction of Σ_1-atoms and ψ a basic concept or role over Σ_2. Given a source ABox A_1 over Σ_1, a target ABox A_2 over Σ_2 is called:
 (i) a solution for (T_1, A_1) under (T_2, M) if (T_1 ∪ T_2 ∪ M, A_1 ∪ A_2) is consistent and every model of (T_1, A_1) together with M can be extended to a model of (T_2, A_2);
 (ii) a universal solution if it is a solution and homomorphically embeds into every other solution;
 (iii) a universal UCQ-solution if it gives the same certain answers as every other solution for every union of conjunctive queries over Σ_2.
A target TBox T_2 is a UCQ-representation of T_1 under M if, for every source ABox A_1 and every UCQ q over Σ_2, the certain answers to q over (T_2, A_2*) coincide with those determined by (T_1 ∪ M, A_1), where A_2* is a canonical target ABox obtained from A_1 via M.

A knowledge-base mapping generation tool (KMGT) is an algorithm that, on input consisting of a source schema/TBox, a target schema/TBox (with target constraints), and possibly correspondences or examples, outputs a finite set M of mapping assertions of the above form.

Problem. Develop a constructive procedure that, given DL-Lite_R source and target TBoxes T_1 and T_2 (with target constraints) together with auxiliary input such as schema correspondences, outputs a mapping M with prescribed formal guarantees drawn from the following list:
 (a) for every source ABox A_1, a universal solution for (T_1, A_1) under (T_2, M) exists and can be effectively computed;
 (b) for every source ABox A_1, a universal UCQ-solution exists and can be effectively computed;
 (c) T_2 is a UCQ-representation of T_1 under M.
Equivalently, characterize precisely which combinations of (a)–(c) can be enforced by a KMGT, give an algorithm that produces such an M when it exists, and prove the algorithm's correctness and complexity bounds. In addition, decide whether there exists a sound and complete KMGT that, taking (T_1, T_2, target constraints, correspondences) as input, always outputs a mapping M satisfying a user-specified subset of the formal properties (a)–(c), whenever such an M exists.

